WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.166 --> 00:00:07.966
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:07.966 --> 00:00:09.896
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:09.896 --> 00:00:12.686
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:12.696 --> 00:00:14.386
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.386 --> 00:00:16.946
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:16.956 --> 00:00:18.936
things about the world at the same

00:00:18.936 --> 00:00:20.776
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.296 --> 00:00:24.356
I'm Alastair Budge, and today we are

00:00:24.356 --> 00:00:27.236
going to talk about one of the most deadly

00:00:27.236 --> 00:00:30.476
diseases in human history: tuberculosis.

00:00:31.016 --> 00:00:33.576
It's a story of science, of social

00:00:33.576 --> 00:00:36.586
class, of romanticism, and of the

00:00:36.636 --> 00:00:39.346
extraordinary way a deadly disease

00:00:39.576 --> 00:00:41.846
came to be seen as something beautiful.

00:00:42.616 --> 00:00:44.526
OK then, let's not waste a

00:00:44.536 --> 00:00:47.062
minute and get right into it.

00:00:48.634 --> 00:00:51.216
It is the winter of 1820,

00:00:51.566 --> 00:00:54.096
and a young English poet is

00:00:54.096 --> 00:00:56.316
coughing into his handkerchief.

00:00:57.066 --> 00:00:59.316
He looks at what he has produced.

00:00:59.806 --> 00:01:01.440
He sees blood.

00:01:01.856 --> 00:01:03.238
Bright red blood.

00:01:04.056 --> 00:01:06.226
He recognises it immediately.

00:01:06.686 --> 00:01:08.436
He has seen it before, in his

00:01:08.436 --> 00:01:10.686
brother, who died of the same

00:01:10.686 --> 00:01:12.306
disease two years earlier.

00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:14.666
"I know the colour of that

00:01:14.666 --> 00:01:16.536
blood", he says to a friend.

00:01:17.056 --> 00:01:19.576
"It is arterial blood;—I cannot

00:01:19.586 --> 00:01:21.239
be deceived by that colour"

00:01:21.856 --> 00:01:24.146
"That drop of blood is my

00:01:24.146 --> 00:01:27.276
death-warrant; I must die."

00:01:28.376 --> 00:01:30.136
The man is the famous

00:01:30.166 --> 00:01:32.364
Romantic poet, John Keats.

00:01:32.686 --> 00:01:34.236
He is twenty-five years old.

00:01:35.206 --> 00:01:36.416
He is not wrong.

00:01:36.906 --> 00:01:38.976
He will be dead within the year.

00:01:40.196 --> 00:01:42.876
Keats was just one of the millions of

00:01:42.906 --> 00:01:45.436
people who would die that year from

00:01:45.436 --> 00:01:48.116
the disease, a disease that was at

00:01:48.116 --> 00:01:51.236
its peak responsible for one in four

00:01:51.236 --> 00:01:54.916
deaths in Europe, and still kills more

00:01:54.956 --> 00:01:57.326
than a million people per year today.

00:01:58.416 --> 00:02:00.376
And here is the strange thing

00:02:00.386 --> 00:02:02.486
about this story, and about the

00:02:02.486 --> 00:02:05.186
peculiar nature of tuberculosis.

00:02:05.726 --> 00:02:07.686
Across Europe at this time,

00:02:08.126 --> 00:02:10.226
people were reading the poetry

00:02:10.226 --> 00:02:12.466
of men like Keats and weeping.

00:02:13.146 --> 00:02:14.776
Not just at the beauty of his

00:02:14.776 --> 00:02:17.866
words, of his poetry, but at the

00:02:17.896 --> 00:02:20.756
supposed beauty of his suffering.

00:02:21.156 --> 00:02:23.816
At the romance of his decline.

00:02:24.846 --> 00:02:26.976
Unlike diseases that had come before

00:02:26.976 --> 00:02:29.326
it, things like the Black Death,

00:02:29.796 --> 00:02:32.586
there was something different, almost

00:02:32.866 --> 00:02:36.446
admirable, enviable, about tuberculosis.

00:02:37.126 --> 00:02:41.306
The pale skin, the thinness, the bright

00:02:41.654 --> 00:02:44.826
feverish eyes, the sense of a person

00:02:44.836 --> 00:02:48.186
burning intensely and briefly, like

00:02:48.186 --> 00:02:51.276
a candle: these were considered by

00:02:51.296 --> 00:02:54.356
some to be marks of a sensitive soul.

00:02:54.676 --> 00:02:56.826
Of an artistic nature.

00:02:57.176 --> 00:03:00.036
Of someone too refined and too

00:03:00.036 --> 00:03:03.396
alive for the dull material world.

00:03:04.276 --> 00:03:05.786
Today, we are going to

00:03:05.786 --> 00:03:07.826
explore how that happened.

00:03:09.056 --> 00:03:11.746
Now, although most people associate

00:03:11.806 --> 00:03:14.076
tuberculosis with the nineteenth

00:03:14.086 --> 00:03:16.546
century, tuberculosis has been

00:03:16.556 --> 00:03:19.286
around for centuries, millennia even.

00:03:20.206 --> 00:03:22.006
There's evidence of it in

00:03:22.006 --> 00:03:24.056
Egyptian mummies, in ancient

00:03:24.066 --> 00:03:26.786
Rome, ancient India and China.

00:03:27.526 --> 00:03:30.056
It has always been there, but it

00:03:30.056 --> 00:03:32.826
wasn't until the late 18th century

00:03:33.206 --> 00:03:35.166
that it really reached its peak.

00:03:36.066 --> 00:03:37.476
For this, we have the

00:03:37.486 --> 00:03:39.426
Industrial Revolution to thank.

00:03:40.306 --> 00:03:43.156
From the late eighteenth century, starting

00:03:43.156 --> 00:03:45.446
in northern England and later spreading

00:03:45.446 --> 00:03:48.736
through Europe, millions of people left

00:03:48.776 --> 00:03:51.706
the countryside and moved to cities.

00:03:52.456 --> 00:03:54.436
They moved because the cities

00:03:54.596 --> 00:03:56.626
were where the work was, in the

00:03:56.626 --> 00:03:59.006
factories and mills and foundries

00:03:59.136 --> 00:04:00.966
that were springing up everywhere.

00:04:01.596 --> 00:04:03.366
The promise of wages.

00:04:03.376 --> 00:04:05.026
The promise of something better

00:04:05.276 --> 00:04:07.226
than the subsistence living they

00:04:07.226 --> 00:04:09.066
were eking out in the fields.

00:04:10.216 --> 00:04:13.136
What they found, in many cases, was

00:04:13.136 --> 00:04:16.436
this: twelve, fourteen, sixteen-hour

00:04:16.436 --> 00:04:19.106
working days in rooms with no

00:04:19.136 --> 00:04:21.626
natural light and no ventilation.

00:04:22.236 --> 00:04:25.626
Housing that was not really housing, more

00:04:25.626 --> 00:04:28.986
like stacked wooden boxes, each containing

00:04:28.986 --> 00:04:31.486
a family, each sharing walls and

00:04:31.486 --> 00:04:34.986
ceilings and air with six other families.

00:04:35.586 --> 00:04:38.226
Streets so narrow that sunlight

00:04:38.446 --> 00:04:40.166
barely reached the ground.

00:04:41.046 --> 00:04:44.176
This was the perfect breeding ground

00:04:44.386 --> 00:04:48.246
for tuberculosis, TB, or the White

00:04:48.286 --> 00:04:50.316
Plague, as it would come to be called.

00:04:51.696 --> 00:04:54.746
Now, tuberculosis is a contagious

00:04:54.746 --> 00:04:57.846
disease caused by a particular type of

00:04:57.846 --> 00:05:01.266
bacterium, Mycobacterium tuberculosis,

00:05:01.496 --> 00:05:03.186
to give it its official name.

00:05:04.526 --> 00:05:06.706
It spreads through the air.

00:05:07.536 --> 00:05:09.806
When an infected person coughs,

00:05:10.186 --> 00:05:13.356
sneezes, or simply breathes, tiny

00:05:13.356 --> 00:05:15.076
droplets carrying the bacteria

00:05:15.406 --> 00:05:17.736
disperse into the surrounding air.

00:05:19.246 --> 00:05:22.396
In a well-ventilated space, or outside,

00:05:22.856 --> 00:05:26.166
the droplets disperse and become harmless.

00:05:26.826 --> 00:05:29.446
In a packed, airless factory or a

00:05:29.476 --> 00:05:32.036
crowded tenement room, they do not.

00:05:33.466 --> 00:05:35.286
The bacterium is passed

00:05:35.296 --> 00:05:36.936
from one person to another.

00:05:37.586 --> 00:05:39.406
Perhaps interestingly enough,

00:05:40.026 --> 00:05:43.166
90-95% of people who are infected

00:05:43.486 --> 00:05:46.726
do not become sick, and instead, the

00:05:46.736 --> 00:05:49.876
bacteria remain inactive in the body.

00:05:50.836 --> 00:05:54.206
This is called latent tuberculosis, and

00:05:54.416 --> 00:05:57.436
it's estimated that even today, around a

00:05:57.496 --> 00:06:00.673
quarter of the world's population have it.

00:06:01.336 --> 00:06:06.316
But for 5-10% of those infected, it turns

00:06:06.516 --> 00:06:10.916
into active tuberculosis, and for much of

00:06:10.926 --> 00:06:14.606
human history, that was a death sentence.

00:06:16.086 --> 00:06:19.476
In most cases, it affects the lungs,

00:06:19.636 --> 00:06:22.486
but it can also spread to other organs.

00:06:23.846 --> 00:06:26.466
The infected person will typically

00:06:26.656 --> 00:06:29.186
cough up blood and feel short of breath,

00:06:29.556 --> 00:06:32.066
they'll have high fever, they will lose

00:06:32.066 --> 00:06:34.976
weight, and for most of human history,

00:06:35.306 --> 00:06:38.076
this 'wasting away' would typically

00:06:38.076 --> 00:06:40.986
last anywhere from two to five years

00:06:41.236 --> 00:06:43.706
before the body finally succumbed.

00:06:45.276 --> 00:06:47.656
And by the middle of the 19th

00:06:47.656 --> 00:06:50.086
century, it was responsible for

00:06:50.086 --> 00:06:52.566
one out of every four deaths.

00:06:53.316 --> 00:06:54.956
It did not discriminate.

00:06:55.386 --> 00:06:56.966
It killed factory workers

00:06:57.316 --> 00:06:58.646
and it killed kings.

00:06:58.856 --> 00:07:00.636
It killed the young and the

00:07:00.636 --> 00:07:02.576
old, the healthy and the

00:07:02.576 --> 00:07:04.756
weak, the rich and the poor.

00:07:05.356 --> 00:07:08.726
It was so widespread it became simply

00:07:08.726 --> 00:07:11.306
a fact of life, as ordinary and

00:07:11.306 --> 00:07:15.096
expected as poverty or cold winters.

00:07:16.426 --> 00:07:18.396
Doctors did not know what

00:07:18.456 --> 00:07:20.516
caused it, or how to cure it.

00:07:21.706 --> 00:07:24.653
Because the disease was so widespread

00:07:24.856 --> 00:07:28.046
and so apparently random, striking some

00:07:28.046 --> 00:07:30.396
people and sparing others who lived in

00:07:30.426 --> 00:07:33.556
identical conditions, doctors didn't

00:07:33.586 --> 00:07:36.056
initially believe it was contagious.

00:07:37.196 --> 00:07:39.146
Two alternative explanations

00:07:39.336 --> 00:07:40.936
rose to fill the gap.

00:07:41.926 --> 00:07:44.576
The first was predisposition.

00:07:45.386 --> 00:07:47.176
Some people, it was believed,

00:07:47.526 --> 00:07:50.186
simply came from families prone

00:07:50.256 --> 00:07:52.406
to consumption, likely to get it.

00:07:53.196 --> 00:07:55.636
If your mother or sister had died

00:07:55.636 --> 00:07:57.776
of it, you were likely to follow.

00:07:58.706 --> 00:08:02.366
Now, this was not entirely wrong, and

00:08:02.566 --> 00:08:05.296
there is some link between genetics and

00:08:05.466 --> 00:08:07.916
likelihood of developing the disease.

00:08:08.566 --> 00:08:10.946
But it also gave doctors a

00:08:10.996 --> 00:08:13.206
convenient excuse — "well, you

00:08:13.206 --> 00:08:15.138
were always bound to get it".

00:08:16.316 --> 00:08:18.446
The second explanation was

00:08:18.446 --> 00:08:20.396
what they called excitement.

00:08:21.016 --> 00:08:24.076
Not excitement in an emotional sense,

00:08:24.076 --> 00:08:27.116
but in a medical sense: the idea

00:08:27.116 --> 00:08:29.646
that certain activities or conditions

00:08:29.916 --> 00:08:32.556
could trigger or worsen the disease

00:08:32.726 --> 00:08:35.076
in those who were predisposed to it.

00:08:36.086 --> 00:08:38.176
And the list of possible

00:08:38.556 --> 00:08:40.786
excitants, or things that could

00:08:40.836 --> 00:08:43.656
cause TB, was extraordinary.

00:08:44.716 --> 00:08:45.756
Working too hard.

00:08:46.076 --> 00:08:47.026
Not working hard enough.

00:08:47.276 --> 00:08:48.226
Sleeping too much.

00:08:48.586 --> 00:08:49.306
Sleeping too little.

00:08:49.876 --> 00:08:50.856
Eating too much.

00:08:50.946 --> 00:08:51.946
Not eating enough.

00:08:52.346 --> 00:08:53.306
Being too cold.

00:08:53.596 --> 00:08:54.556
Being too warm.

00:08:54.916 --> 00:08:56.026
Reading too much.

00:08:56.226 --> 00:08:57.716
Doing too little exercise,

00:08:57.826 --> 00:08:59.226
or too much of it.

00:08:59.836 --> 00:09:02.066
And wearing the wrong clothes.

00:09:03.196 --> 00:09:06.196
The concern, broadly, was about too

00:09:06.206 --> 00:09:08.776
much or too little clothing, and

00:09:09.026 --> 00:09:11.316
there has been much commentary on the

00:09:11.316 --> 00:09:14.136
link between ideas about tuberculosis

00:09:14.196 --> 00:09:15.806
and the fashion of the time.

00:09:16.676 --> 00:09:19.516
Too little clothing exposed the body

00:09:19.676 --> 00:09:22.356
to cold and damp, which was thought to

00:09:22.386 --> 00:09:24.706
disrupt the circulation of the blood and

00:09:24.726 --> 00:09:27.136
allow disease to settle in the lungs.

00:09:27.806 --> 00:09:29.516
Off-the-shoulder styles that

00:09:29.706 --> 00:09:31.736
became fashionable in the 1820s and

00:09:31.736 --> 00:09:34.956
1830s were condemned for exposing

00:09:34.956 --> 00:09:37.016
the collarbone and neckline.

00:09:37.686 --> 00:09:39.976
Thin stockings could also be a problem.

00:09:40.536 --> 00:09:42.856
Even the dropping of high men's

00:09:42.856 --> 00:09:45.356
waistcoats was a cause for concern.

00:09:46.089 --> 00:09:48.369
Too much clothing was equally

00:09:48.379 --> 00:09:50.839
problematic, and for the opposite reason.

00:09:51.599 --> 00:09:54.009
Too many layers were thought to trap

00:09:54.199 --> 00:09:56.979
the body's impurities inside, preventing

00:09:56.979 --> 00:09:59.749
the skin from expelling what it should.

00:10:00.709 --> 00:10:03.319
Of course, all of this was rubbish,

00:10:03.399 --> 00:10:05.539
it was medically incorrect.

00:10:05.949 --> 00:10:08.669
But it's quite revealing about the way

00:10:08.834 --> 00:10:10.779
Victorians thought about the connection

00:10:10.949 --> 00:10:14.469
between disease, behaviour, and morality.

00:10:16.199 --> 00:10:18.769
And the other thing to mention is that

00:10:18.779 --> 00:10:21.309
there is one school of thought, and

00:10:21.339 --> 00:10:23.949
quite a prominent book, that suggests

00:10:23.979 --> 00:10:26.759
that tuberculosis, and the physical

00:10:26.789 --> 00:10:29.939
effects of it, actually shaped fashion

00:10:30.289 --> 00:10:32.789
in the 18th and 19th century, that

00:10:32.789 --> 00:10:35.329
people — especially women — would

00:10:35.409 --> 00:10:38.019
do their make up in a way that made

00:10:38.019 --> 00:10:40.759
them look like they had tuberculosis.

00:10:41.329 --> 00:10:44.029
Pale skin, red lips, and even

00:10:44.029 --> 00:10:46.459
using toxic cosmetics to make

00:10:46.459 --> 00:10:48.179
their pupils more dilated.

00:10:49.169 --> 00:10:51.389
However, there are other historians

00:10:51.649 --> 00:10:53.209
who say that this connection is

00:10:53.439 --> 00:10:56.169
exaggerated, and that tuberculosis

00:10:56.379 --> 00:10:59.159
simply coincided with pre-existing

00:10:59.159 --> 00:11:01.359
fashion trends; it didn't cause them.

00:11:02.879 --> 00:11:05.049
What is agreed upon, however,

00:11:05.339 --> 00:11:07.119
is that the disease did have

00:11:07.119 --> 00:11:08.999
a glamorous quality to it.

00:11:09.599 --> 00:11:11.099
It didn't cover you with

00:11:11.109 --> 00:11:13.169
warts or turn you mad.

00:11:13.539 --> 00:11:15.639
It made you thin and pale,

00:11:15.889 --> 00:11:17.979
your cheeks rosy, your lips

00:11:17.979 --> 00:11:20.289
red, and your eyes sparkly.

00:11:21.529 --> 00:11:23.909
Lord Byron once said he wished

00:11:23.909 --> 00:11:25.399
he could die of consumption,

00:11:25.739 --> 00:11:27.549
because the ladies would say how

00:11:27.549 --> 00:11:29.459
interesting he looked in dying.

00:11:30.179 --> 00:11:32.289
And he spent considerable effort

00:11:32.599 --> 00:11:35.619
cultivating the aesthetic: the pallor,

00:11:35.619 --> 00:11:39.269
the thinness, the air of tragic intensity.

00:11:40.409 --> 00:11:43.269
What's more, the disease often took

00:11:43.269 --> 00:11:46.049
on an even a semi-religious quality.

00:11:46.956 --> 00:11:50.649
Death came slowly, typically over the

00:11:50.649 --> 00:11:53.359
course of months and years, not days.

00:11:54.289 --> 00:11:57.009
There was time for farewells, for

00:11:57.099 --> 00:11:59.569
forgiveness, for the expressions

00:11:59.699 --> 00:12:01.879
of comfort that both the dying and

00:12:01.879 --> 00:12:03.689
the grieving desperately needed.

00:12:04.699 --> 00:12:07.319
Physically, it was almost as if you

00:12:07.319 --> 00:12:10.189
could see the life drifting away from the

00:12:10.219 --> 00:12:13.389
bed-ridden patient, the body wasting away.

00:12:13.829 --> 00:12:16.099
The eyes large and luminous.

00:12:16.279 --> 00:12:19.139
A fever giving a certain intensity

00:12:19.139 --> 00:12:20.449
and brightness to everything.

00:12:21.539 --> 00:12:23.569
And a calmness.

00:12:23.909 --> 00:12:27.759
Even a serenity, as if this caused

00:12:27.779 --> 00:12:29.999
the dying person to accept their

00:12:29.999 --> 00:12:33.169
fate in a way other diseases did not.

00:12:34.329 --> 00:12:37.609
In their final hours, the patient existed

00:12:37.619 --> 00:12:40.169
somewhere between the living and the dead.

00:12:40.619 --> 00:12:42.809
Closer to heaven than any healthy

00:12:42.809 --> 00:12:46.709
person could be, half human, half angel.

00:12:47.589 --> 00:12:50.389
Now, so far we have really been talking

00:12:50.399 --> 00:12:53.759
about the romanticism of tuberculosis by

00:12:53.769 --> 00:12:56.489
a certain class of people: the wealthy,

00:12:56.549 --> 00:12:59.009
the leisured, the people who had the

00:12:59.029 --> 00:13:01.719
time and means to write poetry and

00:13:02.011 --> 00:13:04.709
gad about from townhouse to townhouse.

00:13:05.599 --> 00:13:07.409
For the majority of people who

00:13:07.409 --> 00:13:10.259
died of tuberculosis, there was of

00:13:10.259 --> 00:13:12.149
course nothing romantic about it.

00:13:12.789 --> 00:13:15.399
Factory workers dying alone in a

00:13:15.409 --> 00:13:18.169
crowded apartment, children dying in a

00:13:18.189 --> 00:13:21.359
workhouse, buried in an unmarked grave.

00:13:22.029 --> 00:13:23.699
These were the majority,

00:13:24.269 --> 00:13:25.319
forgotten to history.

00:13:26.639 --> 00:13:30.269
By the middle of the 19th century, TB was

00:13:30.269 --> 00:13:33.839
killing 1% of London's population every

00:13:33.839 --> 00:13:38.179
single year, and was responsible for 40%

00:13:38.179 --> 00:13:40.999
of all deaths of working-class people.

00:13:42.429 --> 00:13:45.779
But by the 1870s, the science

00:13:45.819 --> 00:13:47.789
of disease was changing.

00:13:48.839 --> 00:13:51.719
The old explanations — predisposition,

00:13:51.979 --> 00:13:54.929
excitement, bad air, moral weakness

00:13:55.389 --> 00:13:57.109
— they were rightly beginning to be

00:13:57.109 --> 00:14:00.789
dismissed, to be replaced by germ theory.

00:14:02.049 --> 00:14:04.649
This was the idea that many diseases

00:14:04.829 --> 00:14:08.159
were caused by specific living organisms,

00:14:08.389 --> 00:14:11.189
invisible to the naked eye, that invaded

00:14:11.189 --> 00:14:13.762
the body and reproduced within it.

00:14:15.259 --> 00:14:18.199
Robert Koch, a German doctor, was one of

00:14:18.199 --> 00:14:20.879
the great figures of this new science.

00:14:21.399 --> 00:14:26.279
And on the 24th of March, 1882, he stood

00:14:26.519 --> 00:14:29.399
before the Berlin Physiological Society

00:14:29.689 --> 00:14:32.429
and delivered one of the most important

00:14:32.659 --> 00:14:34.829
scientific announcements in history.

00:14:36.069 --> 00:14:38.769
He had found the bacterium

00:14:39.009 --> 00:14:40.899
that caused tuberculosis.

00:14:41.726 --> 00:14:44.699
Mycobacterium tuberculosis, he called it.

00:14:46.089 --> 00:14:48.819
Over the preceding months, Koch had

00:14:48.829 --> 00:14:50.879
developed entirely new laboratory

00:14:50.879 --> 00:14:54.089
techniques to isolate it, he had grown

00:14:54.089 --> 00:14:56.809
it in culture outside the body, he had

00:14:56.829 --> 00:14:59.579
infected healthy animals with it, and

00:14:59.679 --> 00:15:02.389
he had watched them develop the disease.

00:15:03.069 --> 00:15:05.719
He had proved, conclusively, that

00:15:05.719 --> 00:15:08.349
tuberculosis was not hereditary.

00:15:08.559 --> 00:15:11.289
Not caused by cold air or moral

00:15:11.289 --> 00:15:13.869
weakness or wearing the wrong stockings.

00:15:14.319 --> 00:15:15.889
It was an infection.

00:15:16.329 --> 00:15:17.319
A bacterium.

00:15:17.759 --> 00:15:20.249
Something that could, in principle,

00:15:20.549 --> 00:15:22.789
be understood and beaten.

00:15:24.379 --> 00:15:27.599
Koch won the Nobel Prize for his efforts.

00:15:28.039 --> 00:15:30.049
He had discovered what caused

00:15:30.109 --> 00:15:34.019
tuberculosis, but, importantly, this did

00:15:34.019 --> 00:15:36.929
not equal discovering how to treat it.

00:15:37.979 --> 00:15:40.399
No drug capable of killing the

00:15:40.449 --> 00:15:42.949
tuberculosis bacterium could be found.

00:15:43.719 --> 00:15:47.529
The organism was unusual: slow-growing

00:15:47.769 --> 00:15:51.059
and protected by a waxy outer coat that

00:15:51.069 --> 00:15:53.614
most substances could not penetrate.

00:15:54.449 --> 00:15:57.189
What grew up instead was something called

00:15:57.399 --> 00:16:00.279
the sanitarium, a type of specialised

00:16:00.289 --> 00:16:03.019
hospital, typically far from a city,

00:16:03.359 --> 00:16:05.799
near the sea or up in the mountains.

00:16:07.109 --> 00:16:09.259
The logic was straightforward.

00:16:09.869 --> 00:16:12.019
It was the urban environment where

00:16:12.019 --> 00:16:13.819
tuberculosis flourished, with

00:16:13.839 --> 00:16:16.459
its crowding, its pollution, its

00:16:16.469 --> 00:16:18.329
lack of sunlight and fresh air.

00:16:18.959 --> 00:16:21.409
So removing the patient from that

00:16:21.409 --> 00:16:23.859
environment might allow the body's

00:16:23.929 --> 00:16:26.399
own defences to fight the disease.

00:16:27.819 --> 00:16:29.789
Sanitaria spread across

00:16:29.809 --> 00:16:31.349
Europe and North America.

00:16:31.949 --> 00:16:33.669
They were built high in the Alps

00:16:33.849 --> 00:16:35.679
and the Scottish Highlands, on the

00:16:35.699 --> 00:16:37.836
coasts of southern Europe, in the

00:16:37.836 --> 00:16:40.296
mountain ranges of the American west.

00:16:41.046 --> 00:16:43.216
Patients were given beds on

00:16:43.276 --> 00:16:45.606
open-air terraces, even in winter.

00:16:46.196 --> 00:16:47.756
They were encouraged to sleep

00:16:47.776 --> 00:16:50.086
outdoors, to breathe deeply,

00:16:50.456 --> 00:16:52.246
to eat large amounts of food.

00:16:53.266 --> 00:16:55.866
Some facilities recommended as many

00:16:55.886 --> 00:16:58.206
as six thousand calories a day,

00:16:58.586 --> 00:17:00.996
which is about three times the daily

00:17:00.996 --> 00:17:03.866
recommendation for an adult woman, or

00:17:03.996 --> 00:17:07.066
for the McDonald's fans, 11 Big Macs.

00:17:08.216 --> 00:17:10.256
For those who could afford the better

00:17:10.296 --> 00:17:12.786
establishments, a sanitarium could

00:17:12.786 --> 00:17:16.176
be almost pleasant: a long rest in

00:17:16.186 --> 00:17:18.396
beautiful surroundings with regular

00:17:18.396 --> 00:17:20.416
meals and medical supervision.

00:17:21.776 --> 00:17:23.876
For the poor in the public

00:17:23.876 --> 00:17:26.396
sanitaria, the experience was

00:17:26.396 --> 00:17:27.856
something closer to a prison.

00:17:28.616 --> 00:17:30.076
You were removed from your

00:17:30.076 --> 00:17:32.606
family, your work, your life.

00:17:33.126 --> 00:17:35.876
You might stay for months or years.

00:17:36.946 --> 00:17:39.396
And unfortunately, the sanitaria

00:17:39.696 --> 00:17:41.346
didn't work particularly well,

00:17:41.596 --> 00:17:43.186
whether private or public.

00:17:44.246 --> 00:17:46.426
Rest and good food could

00:17:46.436 --> 00:17:47.706
strengthen a patient.

00:17:48.266 --> 00:17:50.016
Clean air reduced the spread

00:17:50.016 --> 00:17:51.176
of infection to others.

00:17:51.946 --> 00:17:54.386
But for those already seriously

00:17:54.426 --> 00:17:58.296
ill, the "cure" was often temporary.

00:17:58.966 --> 00:18:01.986
Many would stay for years, seemingly

00:18:01.986 --> 00:18:04.296
improving, only to relapse and

00:18:04.306 --> 00:18:06.376
die the moment they returned to

00:18:06.376 --> 00:18:08.506
the smog and poverty of the city.

00:18:10.076 --> 00:18:11.876
There was a step forward with

00:18:11.876 --> 00:18:14.736
the BCG vaccine, which was first

00:18:14.736 --> 00:18:18.236
administered in 1921, but while it

00:18:18.246 --> 00:18:20.566
reduced the risk of severe disease;

00:18:20.836 --> 00:18:23.456
it did not eliminate tuberculosis.

00:18:24.846 --> 00:18:28.109
The real answer arrived in 1943,

00:18:28.576 --> 00:18:31.536
in a soil laboratory in New Jersey.

00:18:32.926 --> 00:18:36.036
A scientist named Selman Waksman had

00:18:36.036 --> 00:18:39.176
spent years studying the microorganisms

00:18:39.326 --> 00:18:42.086
that live in soil, reasoning that if

00:18:42.106 --> 00:18:44.836
bacteria can destroy each other in nature,

00:18:45.406 --> 00:18:48.176
some of them might produce substances

00:18:48.366 --> 00:18:50.546
that could be useful as medicines.

00:18:51.906 --> 00:18:53.886
He and his team tested

00:18:54.036 --> 00:18:55.886
thousands of soil samples.

00:18:56.676 --> 00:18:58.286
Most produced nothing.

00:18:59.276 --> 00:19:01.586
Then they found one that did.

00:19:02.486 --> 00:19:04.926
A common soil bacterium produced a

00:19:04.926 --> 00:19:07.956
substance that could kill Mycobacterium

00:19:07.956 --> 00:19:09.719
tuberculosis in the laboratory.

00:19:10.736 --> 00:19:12.954
They called it streptomycin.

00:19:14.356 --> 00:19:17.504
On the 20th of November, 1944,

00:19:17.912 --> 00:19:20.626
streptomycin was given for the first time

00:19:20.896 --> 00:19:23.826
to a human patient: a young woman who

00:19:23.826 --> 00:19:26.486
was critically ill with tuberculosis and

00:19:26.486 --> 00:19:29.166
had been expected to die within weeks.

00:19:30.206 --> 00:19:32.466
Miraculously, she recovered.

00:19:33.766 --> 00:19:35.716
The response in the medical world

00:19:35.916 --> 00:19:38.386
was something close to disbelief.

00:19:39.126 --> 00:19:41.816
Here was a drug that could actually cure

00:19:41.846 --> 00:19:44.356
a disease that had killed large parts

00:19:44.356 --> 00:19:46.766
of the population for two centuries.

00:19:47.836 --> 00:19:49.766
Waksman received the Nobel

00:19:49.766 --> 00:19:52.199
Prize in Medicine in 1952.

00:19:53.576 --> 00:19:54.966
Over the following decades,

00:19:55.176 --> 00:19:56.946
more antibiotics followed.

00:19:57.416 --> 00:19:59.566
TB wards began to empty.

00:19:59.986 --> 00:20:01.436
Sanitaria closed.

00:20:01.986 --> 00:20:04.066
In the wealthy countries of Europe

00:20:04.216 --> 00:20:06.376
and North America, tuberculosis

00:20:06.646 --> 00:20:08.316
became increasingly rare.

00:20:09.096 --> 00:20:12.416
And by the 1980s, some public health

00:20:12.416 --> 00:20:14.686
officials were quietly talking

00:20:14.906 --> 00:20:17.206
about eliminating it entirely.

00:20:18.276 --> 00:20:20.006
But, as you may know,

00:20:20.366 --> 00:20:22.756
tuberculosis has never gone away.

00:20:23.826 --> 00:20:26.006
While the developed world was

00:20:26.006 --> 00:20:28.326
congratulating itself on all but

00:20:28.326 --> 00:20:31.056
defeating it, the disease continued

00:20:31.066 --> 00:20:32.516
in the parts of the world that

00:20:32.536 --> 00:20:34.666
could not afford antibiotics, could

00:20:34.886 --> 00:20:37.206
not build hospitals, and could

00:20:37.206 --> 00:20:39.216
not fund public health campaigns.

00:20:40.266 --> 00:20:43.446
Today, tuberculosis still infects

00:20:43.486 --> 00:20:46.846
around ten million people every year.

00:20:48.066 --> 00:20:51.486
In 2024, it killed approximately

00:20:51.576 --> 00:20:55.156
1.25 million people, making it

00:20:55.186 --> 00:20:57.196
one of the leading infectious

00:20:57.196 --> 00:20:59.206
disease killers on the planet.

00:21:00.526 --> 00:21:02.466
The vast majority of those deaths

00:21:02.696 --> 00:21:05.246
occur in South-East Asia, sub-Saharan

00:21:05.246 --> 00:21:07.766
Africa, and the Western Pacific, in

00:21:07.766 --> 00:21:09.676
conditions of poverty and overcrowding

00:21:10.046 --> 00:21:11.656
that would have been familiar to the

00:21:11.666 --> 00:21:14.536
factory workers of Manchester in 1840.

00:21:15.436 --> 00:21:18.256
And now there is an additional problem

00:21:18.476 --> 00:21:20.206
making the situation considerably

00:21:20.206 --> 00:21:23.266
worse: drug-resistant tuberculosis.

00:21:24.196 --> 00:21:26.616
This happens when antibiotics are

00:21:26.616 --> 00:21:29.246
used incorrectly — when courses

00:21:29.246 --> 00:21:30.986
of treatment are interrupted,

00:21:31.246 --> 00:21:33.096
people are given the wrong doses,

00:21:33.346 --> 00:21:35.426
or lower quality drugs are used.

00:21:36.086 --> 00:21:39.256
In these cases, the tuberculosis bacteria

00:21:39.476 --> 00:21:43.326
can evolve to resist the drugs, and

00:21:43.566 --> 00:21:49.376
in 2024, an estimated 390,000 people

00:21:49.796 --> 00:21:52.086
developed drug-resistant tuberculosis.

00:21:52.856 --> 00:21:55.466
Treating it requires different, more

00:21:55.466 --> 00:21:58.226
expensive regimens, with more severe side

00:21:58.226 --> 00:22:01.456
effects and much lower success rates.

00:22:02.696 --> 00:22:06.736
Of those 390,000 people, only

00:22:07.036 --> 00:22:09.306
around two in five were able

00:22:09.306 --> 00:22:11.336
to access any treatment at all.

00:22:12.956 --> 00:22:15.266
So the unfortunate point we need

00:22:15.266 --> 00:22:18.456
to end with is this: Tuberculosis

00:22:18.706 --> 00:22:21.212
has had a cure since 1944.

00:22:21.822 --> 00:22:22.946
The drugs exist.

00:22:23.336 --> 00:22:24.666
The vaccine exists.

00:22:24.976 --> 00:22:26.846
The knowledge of how it spreads,

00:22:26.856 --> 00:22:28.866
and how to prevent it, has been

00:22:28.866 --> 00:22:31.376
established for well over a century.

00:22:31.986 --> 00:22:34.756
It doesn't have to kill anyone, yet it

00:22:34.766 --> 00:22:38.296
still kills 1.25 million people a year.

00:22:39.076 --> 00:22:41.456
And I think we can all agree that

00:22:41.456 --> 00:22:43.856
there is nothing romantic about that.

00:22:45.876 --> 00:22:48.256
OK, then, that is it for today's episode

00:22:48.286 --> 00:22:50.616
on the deadly history of tuberculosis.

00:22:50.746 --> 00:22:52.026
I hope it's been an interesting one

00:22:52.356 --> 00:22:53.816
and that you've learnt something new.

00:22:54.466 --> 00:22:56.566
We've already made a few episodes

00:22:56.566 --> 00:22:58.366
on things related to this episode,

00:22:58.586 --> 00:23:00.216
which you might like to check out

00:23:00.356 --> 00:23:01.626
if you haven't done so already.

00:23:01.986 --> 00:23:03.806
There's an episode on Lord Byron,

00:23:03.986 --> 00:23:06.746
which is episode 244, on the

00:23:06.746 --> 00:23:08.726
Brontë sisters, several of whom

00:23:08.736 --> 00:23:10.576
died of tuberculosis, which is

00:23:10.596 --> 00:23:14.166
episode 271, and on the Industrial

00:23:14.166 --> 00:23:17.246
Revolution, which is episode 150.

00:23:18.056 --> 00:23:19.856
You've been listening to English Learning

00:23:19.856 --> 00:23:22.436
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:23:22.816 --> 00:23:25.656
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:23:25.656 --> 00:23:27.956
I'll catch you in the next episode.

